# /opt/sheaf/env - environment of sheaf.service, the server instance for box 1 (BOX=box1: links 47001/47002, shf0,
# 10.66.0.1/30, key /etc/sheaf/box1.key). Install it as /opt/sheaf/env (not box1.env): sheaf.service reads that file.
# Example of what runs on the test server since 26 Nov (sheaf v61 and later; -auth=half needs v62); no keys or secrets in here.
#
# The unit adds: -reliable -serve -allow 127.0.0.1:5201,127.0.0.1:5202 -key /etc/sheaf/box1.key -tun shf0
#   -links 0.0.0.0:47001>-,0.0.0.0:47002>- -trace=${TRACE} -frame=${FRAME}
# EXTRA:
#   -status          status file (sheaf-server.sh, measurements)
#   -mode peer       follow the box's mode (/etc/sheaf/mode on the box, set in LuCI)
#   -pace 1.25       spread each link's sends at 1.25 x window / srtt
#   -fqlimit 1048576 1 MB flow queue between shf0 and the sender (the default since v59; explicit so a rollback to an older
#                    binary keeps it)
#   -tallow public   the box runs TPROXY=1: allow transparent opens to public addresses (without it every LAN TCP
#                    connection of the box to the internet is refused)
#   -tdeny :25       never dial SMTP for the box's LAN (the server dials these itself, its FORWARD rules do not apply)
#   -auth=half       HalfSipHash datagram tag, as in the box's sheaf.conf (both ends must agree)
#
# sheaf.service runs its own binary /opt/sheaf/sheaf-box; sheaf@hub and sheaf@box2 share /opt/sheaf/sheaf. Replacing
# one changes only the instances that run it, at their next restart. Instances without -tallow refuse transparent
# opens and otherwise behave as before.
# After a change: systemctl restart sheaf (the box resets within seconds).
FRAME=1328
EXTRA=-status /run/sheaf-status.json -mode peer -pace 1.25 -fqlimit 1048576 -tallow public -tdeny :25 -auth=half
TRACE=
